{
    "word": "ardency",
    "lang_code": "en",
    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "d9c1a2ca-807f-3b48-8dc7-6f0b85951efd",
                "word": "ardency",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "238e62b0-7eba-4e08-924c-af2bb599a93b",
                        "entry_id": "d9c1a2ca-807f-3b48-8dc7-6f0b85951efd",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "ardencies"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "af42f646-dd9f-386d-bc22-2e95e79c53f2",
                        "entry_id": "d9c1a2ca-807f-3b48-8dc7-6f0b85951efd",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The quality of being ardent.",
                            "Intensity of feelings."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "e4d6cb88-ab44-4584-bb72-30d1aedf479a",
                                "sense_id": "af42f646-dd9f-386d-bc22-2e95e79c53f2",
                                "text": "So is their ioyes with fearefull passions mixt,\nWhich doth encrease the ardencie of loue,\nOn the forbidden thinges our eyes are fixt;"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "2d01360d-0b91-46c1-8dc6-8d805af738e8",
                                "sense_id": "af42f646-dd9f-386d-bc22-2e95e79c53f2",
                                "text": "The Ardency of my Passion made me incapable of uttering more;"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0c73fe93-c1e2-4dec-8fcb-f0e0afe4f0f1",
                                "sense_id": "af42f646-dd9f-386d-bc22-2e95e79c53f2",
                                "text": "[…] the silver flow\nOf Hero’s tears, the swoon of Imogen,\nFair Pastorella in the bandit’s den,\nAre things to brood on with more ardency\nThan the death-day of empires."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "aa529dc0-d843-4ea5-8d80-c847be860123",
                                "sense_id": "af42f646-dd9f-386d-bc22-2e95e79c53f2",
                                "text": "If there had been in Tom Walsingham’s brain a flame or even a flicker of response to Kit’s poetic ardency or the cunning of his learning, then there would have been other linkings and knottings and the joy of discourse in the cool of after love […]"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "51dbc8a6-491c-3d85-aa41-f17953772bd0",
                        "entry_id": "d9c1a2ca-807f-3b48-8dc7-6f0b85951efd",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The quality of being ardent.",
                            "Intensity of devotion."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "ef92486c-bb82-43a1-9f84-cc760758518f",
                                "sense_id": "51dbc8a6-491c-3d85-aa41-f17953772bd0",
                                "text": "1548, Hugh Latimer, sermon preached on 22 March, 1548 in 27 Sermons, London: John Day, 1562, p. 46,\nHe repayred to God with this prayer, and said nothinge. Yet wyth a great ardency of spirit, he pearsed Gods eares."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "02283d32-706a-4c30-a8b0-f66ad3f0f49f",
                                "sense_id": "51dbc8a6-491c-3d85-aa41-f17953772bd0",
                                "text": "1650, John Milton, letter to John IV of Portugal dated 27 April, 1650, in Letters of State Written by Mr. John Milton, London, 1694, p. ,\nThis, as we have earnestly desired in our former Letters, so now again with the greatest ardency and importunity we request of your Majesty."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8a55901c-6fc2-48ca-9740-06f90a55e677",
                                "sense_id": "51dbc8a6-491c-3d85-aa41-f17953772bd0",
                                "text": "I must try, by excerpts from his diary and correspondence, to convey to the reader some idea of the ardency and thoroughness with which he threw himself into the largest and least of his multifarious engagements in this service."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "4e02c7e1-1185-344a-806e-249a6cb901e0",
                        "entry_id": "d9c1a2ca-807f-3b48-8dc7-6f0b85951efd",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The quality of being ardent.",
                            "Intensity of heat or burning."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "8c001450-bd88-4ed9-a68f-a3545656e298",
                                "sense_id": "4e02c7e1-1185-344a-806e-249a6cb901e0",
                                "text": "1596, Francis Sabie, “David and Beersheba” Adams Complaint, London: Richard Johnes,\nField-tilling Swains driue home their toiling teams,\nOut-wearied with ardencie of heat:"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e9df621d-7645-4605-811b-96e0acd753eb",
                                "sense_id": "4e02c7e1-1185-344a-806e-249a6cb901e0",
                                "text": "The use of clysters is so manifest, so obvious (especially during the ardency of the fever) that I have no further occasion to insist on their being serviceable;"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "dba9b9ec-614c-42b4-8ff4-7c9b19d5b148",
                                "sense_id": "4e02c7e1-1185-344a-806e-249a6cb901e0",
                                "text": "[…] the ardency of the sun grew greater, and the Hermit’s cliff was a fiery furnace."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "4bf7b798-7aaa-3d61-9560-a2b8d990f61b",
                        "entry_id": "d9c1a2ca-807f-3b48-8dc7-6f0b85951efd",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The quality of being ardent.",
                            "Intensity of light."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "4586f626-4eb3-4370-a08e-6bd77f4219dd",
                                "sense_id": "4bf7b798-7aaa-3d61-9560-a2b8d990f61b",
                                "text": "One could not look a moment, without a weeping of the sight, into the blinding ardency of the western atmosphere, so charged was it with the ceaseless gushing of the crimson glory;"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b4e86daf-f2e4-4171-9d4d-324c8bd34f85",
                                "sense_id": "4bf7b798-7aaa-3d61-9560-a2b8d990f61b",
                                "text": "I can shut my eyes now and see that incomparable sunrise; I can see again that vision of mountains filling half the sky with their unimaginable ardency, and I think that this world never presented nobler sight."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
